---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';

const title = 'How to Use';
const description =
  'The full operator manual: wizard mode, CLI invocations, release signing, memory profiles, automation flags, and what the engine changes on your machine.';
const repo = 'C:\\path\\to\\android-apk-builder';
const proj = 'C:\\your-android-project';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} wide>
  <div class="page-head">
    <h1>How to Use</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Two interfaces: <code>build.bat</code> for one-click and command-line use,
      <code>setup-windows.ps1</code> for the native PowerShell engine with every knob.
      Everything below applies on Windows 10 and 11 without Android Studio.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Wizard mode</h2>
  <p>
    Double-click <code>build.bat</code> in the builder folder. Since that folder is not
    itself an Android project, the wizard asks for the target directory and accepts a
    typed path or a drag-and-dropped folder. Then choose:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><kbd>Enter</kbd> builds the default, a Release APK.</li>
    <li><kbd>C</kbd> and <kbd>Enter</kbd> opens customization: Release/Debug/Both,
    APK/AAB/Both, clean build, and low-memory mode.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Drag-and-drop also works directly on <code>build.bat</code> from Windows Explorer,
    building the dropped project immediately. The terminal pauses at the end so you can
    review harvested paths and the signature verification output.
  </p>

  <h2>Command-line invocations</h2>
  <p>Run inside the Android project directory:</p>
  <CodeBlock caption="Build the current project" code={`cd ${proj}\n${repo}\\build.bat -BuildType Release`} />
  <p>Or point at the project from anywhere:</p>
  <CodeBlock caption="Explicit project directory" code={`${repo}\\build.bat\n-ProjectDir "${proj}" -BuildType Release`} />

  <h3>Build variants</h3>
  <CodeBlock caption="Release, Debug, Both" code={`${repo}\\build.bat -BuildType Debug\n${repo}\\build.bat -BuildType Release\n${repo}\\build.bat -BuildType Both`} />

  <h3>Artifact types</h3>
  <p>
    Produce installable APKs, Play Store bundles (.aab), or both. AAB uploads are what
    Google Play expects for publishing; APKs install locally.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock caption="APK, AAB, Both" code={`${repo}\\build.bat -ArtifactType AAB\n${repo}\\build.bat -ArtifactType Both`} />

  <h3>Multi-module apps</h3>
  <p>Repositories with several app modules take a <code>-Module</code>:</p>
  <CodeBlock caption="Target one module" code={`${repo}\\build.bat -Module ":app" -BuildType Release`} />

  <h2>Release signing</h2>
  <p>
    With no keystore supplied, the engine generates a dedicated release keystore,
    signs the APK with v1, v2, and v3 schemes, and stores the keystore for reuse so
    every build keeps the same signature. Provide your own instead:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock caption="Custom keystore" code={`${repo}\\build.bat -KeystorePath "C:\\keys\\app.jks"\n-StorePassword "secret123" -KeyAlias "myalias"`} />
  <p>
    Externally managed signing (for example when Gradle config handles release signing)
    uses <code>-SkipSigning</code>. Certificates can be fully described:
    <code>-KeyDName "CN=Dev,O=Org,C=US"</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Hardware profiles</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt><code>-ForceLowMem</code></dt>
    <dd>
      Caps the JVM near 1.5 GB, single worker, no daemon. For 4 GB and tight 8 GB
      machines, where an unconstrained Gradle can freeze the pagefile.
    </dd>
    <dt><code>-HighMem</code></dt>
    <dd>4 GB heap, parallel workers, build cache. For multi-core workstations.</dd>
    <dt>Default</dt>
    <dd>
      The engine reads physical RAM via WMI and picks a profile automatically.
      Overhead stays under 8.5 GB total: below that, conservative settings apply.
    </dd>
  </dl>
  <CodeBlock caption="Force a profile" code={`${repo}\\build.bat -ForceLowMem\n${repo}\\build.bat -HighMem`} />

  <h2>Automation and CI switches</h2>
  <p>
    For pipelines and unattended runs, combine:
    <code>-AcceptLicenses</code> (non-interactive SDK licenses),
    <code>-CleanFirst</code> (Gradle clean before assembling),
    <code>-OutputDir "C:\builds"</code>, <code>-Offline</code>,
    <code>-DryRun</code> (inspect and plan without compiling), and
    <code>-VerboseLog</code>.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock caption="Headless example" code={`${repo}\\build.bat -ProjectDir "${proj}"\n-AcceptLicenses -CleanFirst -OutputDir "C:\\builds"`} />

  <h2>What the engine changes on your machine</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Environment variables (<code>JAVA_HOME</code>, <code>ANDROID_HOME</code>,
      <code>GRADLE_OPTS</code>) are scoped to the running session. Nothing is written to
      the registry or system-wide environment.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>local.properties</code> gets its <code>sdk.dir</code> line updated with
      forward slashes; every other line, including comments, is preserved, and a
      <code>local.properties.bak</code> backup is created first.
    </li>
    <li>
      Artifacts are copied out of Gradle's build folders, never moved, so incremental
      rebuild caches stay valid.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Outputs and verification</h2>
  <p>
    Harvested files land in the output directory (<code>dist</code> by default) and are
    mirrored to <code>android-apk-builder\dist\&lt;project&gt;</code>. For every artifact the
    engine prints a SHA-256 hash and runs <code>apksigner verify</code>, listing active
    signature schemes. The signing manifest (<code>signing-credentials.txt</code>)
    contains the passwords, alias, certificate fingerprints, a Base64 keystore for CI
    secrets, and a ready GitHub Actions step.
  </p>
  <p>
    Keep generated keystores with the project. Installing an update APK signed with a
    different key fails on-device with
    <code>INSTALL_FAILED_UPDATE_INCOMPATIBLE</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Reporting issues</h2>
  <p>
    If you face any issues, encounter unexpected build failures, or have questions while using Android APK Builder, please
    <a href="https://github.com/tanvirr007/android-apk-builder/issues" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">report it on GitHub</a>.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
